5:[["$","$L10","4.9",{"topic":{"id":"4.9","title":"Preparation of Forensic Reports","minutes":12,"summary":"Turn technical work into a clear, reproducible, source-linked account that a court or another examiner can evaluate.","sections":[{"title":"Essential report components","body":["Identify the instructing authority, examiner, scope, dates, exhibits, and question. Record acquisition and examination methods, tool versions, relevant settings, hashes, and limitations. Present findings in a logical order and connect each material conclusion to a source. Distinguish facts supplied by others from facts the examiner directly verified.","Use appendices for the exhibit register, custody history, hash table, timeline, and detailed technical output. Define acronyms and explain specialized terms. The reader should understand why a result matters without having to trust an unexplained tool label. A concise executive summary should remain faithful to the body of the report."]},{"title":"Writing defensible conclusions","body":["Use calibrated language such as observed, consistent with, insufficient evidence, or cannot be determined. Explain the basis for the chosen level of confidence. Avoid legal conclusions reserved to the court, such as declaring a person guilty. A forensic examiner can describe artifacts and technical implications; proving the offence requires the wider evidential record.","Record contrary evidence and material limitations. If a clock offset is unknown, a tool could not decode an application, or an acquisition had read errors, disclose it. A report is not persuasive because it hides uncertainty. It is persuasive because the reader can see how uncertainty was handled."]},{"title":"Review and controlled issue","body":["Check every exhibit reference, hash, timestamp, calculation, and quotation. Technical review should test the reasoning as well as spelling. Preserve drafts and corrections under the applicable disclosure and records policy. When issuing a supplement, explain what changed and why rather than silently replacing the earlier report.","Create redacted disclosure copies where necessary without altering the original. For classroom PDF export, this course includes the lesson, practical exercise, sources, and optional answer explanations. A real forensic report additionally requires the actual exhibit records, signed documentation, and applicable procedural compliance."]}],"practice":{"title":"Write a finding template","scenario":"A recovered message is linked to a mailbox export but not yet to a human sender.","steps":["State the observed artifact and its source.","Describe the method and verification.","Explain the attribution limitation.","Draft a conclusion that avoids declaring guilt."],"debrief":"A precise finding can be useful without exceeding the evidence. Clear limits help the investigator decide what to collect next."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["bsa","cert","nist-guide"],"labId":"certificate","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain essential report components and apply it to an investigation.","Explain writing defensible conclusions and apply it to an investigation.","Explain review and controlled issue and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.9","prompt":"Which belongs in a defensible forensic report?","options":["Only conclusions that support the prosecution.","Methods, sources, findings, limitations, and reproducible references.","Unsupported claims about the suspect’s character.","A tool name with no version or settings."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The reader must be able to evaluate the process and the basis of the conclusions.","unit":4}}}]]
